  "description": "A parametric LINEAR PROFILE on the six-node spine — Geometry → Input Properties → Math Engine → Output Metrics → 2D Render + 3D Render.\n\nDROP IT ON A SEGMENT. A plaster-in run with no visible trim: the slot, the carrier above it and the lens set back inside.\n\nA LONG RUN IS NOT ONE EXTRUSION. It is modules with joints, and the joints are visible from the floor. Rounding the count and dividing the run back out is what keeps every module the same length — which is what a trimless detail has to be, because an odd short piece at one end is the thing that gives it away.\n\nCUT-OFF FROM NADIR IS TWO NUMBERS. How wide the slot is and how deep the lens sits. The limiting sight line runs from the far edge of the aperture to the near edge of the lens, so `atan2(slot width, recess)` is the angle at which the lens disappears behind the aperture edge, and it is the entire reason to set a lens back. `Darklight` at 60 mm gives 28.8°; a flush opal lens gives 90°, which means visible from everywhere.\n\nTHE OPTIC IS REPORTED, NOT MODELLED. Opal, darklight, microprism and asymmetric are four different photometric distributions and one identical extrusion. The distribution belongs to the lamp file; the extrusion belongs here.\n\nDIMENSIONS AT THE DEFAULTS: a 3 m run of 35 mm slot, 75 mm deep, lens 60 mm back, in three 999 mm modules with 2 mm joints — 28.8° cut-off, 3300 lm.",
